#### 8. Exception Handling in Tests
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError` (line 105)  
**Evidence:**
```csharp
try {
    processor.ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.Fail("Expected an exception but none was thrown");
} catch (ValidationException ex) {
    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```
**Problem:** Manual try/catch duplicates what the framework provides and can mask unexpected exceptions (e.g., a different exception type would be caught by a different handler or slip through).  
**Fix:**
```csharp
var ex = Assert.ThrowsException<ValidationException>(
    () => processor.ProcessOrder(order));
Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
```
**Note:** This is a capture-and-assert pattern (functional but ugly), so severity remains Medium rather than High.

